.TH PASCAL-LLM 1 "" "pascal-llm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pascal-llm \- run local LLMs on a 2016 Pascal GPU under the newest Ubuntu and kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 4
.nf
\&./pascal\-llm steps
\&./pascal\-llm run STEP
\&./pascal\-llm status
\&./pascal\-llm check
\&./pascal\-llm guide
\&./pascal\-llm man [\-\-install | \-\-raw]
\&./pascal\-llm \-\-version
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
pascal-llm-stack is a recipe, proven on one machine, for running local LLMs on a Pascal GPU (a GTX 1060 Mobile with 6 GB) under Ubuntu 26.04 and kernel 7.0: the NVIDIA 570 driver patched for kernel 7.0, CUDA 12.8 (the last branch that compiles \fBsm_61\fR), llama.cpp built for the card, systemd services, a proxy that serves a chat page and an OpenAI style API on port 8080, ComfyUI for images and video, Tailscale serving to your own devices only, and a smoke test that runs after every kernel update. The 7B model serves about 22 tokens a second.
.PP
\fBpascal\-llm\fR is the one entry point to the numbered scripts. Each step is a plain script you can read first; most need sudo, and each says what it does when it starts.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBsteps\fR
List the steps in order, with the script each one runs.
.TP
\fBrun STEP\fR
Run one step. Most need sudo; the driver step reboots.
.TP
\fBstatus\fR
Read only: the kernel module, the GPU, CUDA, the three services and the API on 8080.
.TP
\fBcheck\fR
The smoke test: module, \fBnvidia\-smi\fR, \fBlibcuda\fR against the 570 userspace, a token rate on the GPU.
.TP
\fBguide\fR
Print this manual as Markdown, for people and agents.
.TP
\fBman\fR
Show this manual as a man page; \fB\-\-install\fR makes \fBman pascal\-llm\fR work.
.SH STEPS
.TP
\fBdriver\fR
\fB01\-driver/install\-nvidia\-driver.sh\fR: NVIDIA 570.211.01 with the kernel 7.0 patch, through DKMS.
.TP
\fBbuild\-tools\fR
\fB02\-cuda/setup\-build\-tools.sh\fR: compilers and build tools, without Ubuntu's CUDA package.
.TP
\fBcuda\fR
\fB02\-cuda/install\-cuda\-toolkit.sh\fR: CUDA 12.8.1 from the runfile, toolkit only, with the glibc fix.
.TP
\fBllama\fR
\fB03\-inference/build\-llama\-cpp.sh\fR: llama.cpp for \fBsm_61\fR, run as your own user.
.TP
\fBservices\fR
\fB03\-inference/setup\-services.sh\fR: llama-server, the proxy on 8080 and ComfyUI as systemd services.
.TP
\fBtailscale\fR
\fB04\-tailscale/setup\-tailscale.sh\fR: Tailscale, with \fBtailscale serve\fR to your tailnet only.
.TP
\fBsmoke\-test\fR
\fB05\-checks/install\-smoke\-test.sh\fR: the smoke test after every kernel update and at first boot.
.TP
\fBstability\fR
Optional. \fB02\-cuda/setup\-stability.sh\fR: a 16 GB swapfile and a 70 W power limit; edit the paths first.
.TP
\fBdiffusion\fR
Optional. \fB03\-inference/download\-diffusion.sh\fR: image and video models; edit the paths first.
.TP
\fBbig\-model\fR
Optional. \fB03\-inference/use\-27b.sh\fR: a 27B model at about 2 tokens a second; set \fBMODEL\fR.
.SH SERVICES AND PORTS
.PP
\fBllama\-server\fR listens on 127.0.0.1:8090. \fBllama\-web\fR is the proxy on 8080: a chat page and \fB/v1/*\fR streamed to llama-server, and the one port Tailscale serves. \fBcomfyui\fR listens on 127.0.0.1:8188. llama-server and ComfyUI conflict on purpose, so starting one stops the other and the 6 GB of memory goes to one workload at a time.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.PP
\fBMODEL\fR is the GGUF file for the services and the 27B switch. \fBCOMFY_LISTEN\fR is ComfyUI's listen address, 127.0.0.1 by default; ComfyUI has no login, so open it to the network only on purpose. \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR moves the man folder \fBman \-\-install\fR writes to.
.SH FILES
.PP
The smoke test logs to the journal (\fBjournalctl \-u pascal\-smoke\-test\fR, tag \fBpascal\-smoke\fR) and keeps its state in \fB/var/lib/pascal\-smoke\fR; a failed DKMS build is explained in \fB/var/lib/dkms/nvidia/<version>/build/make.log\fR. The services are \fB/etc/systemd/system/llama\-server.service\fR, \fBllama\-web.service\fR and \fBcomfyui.service\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
0 on success. 2 for an unknown command or step. \fBcheck\fR exits non zero when any part of the stack fails, and a \fBrun\fR exits with its script's status.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
From a fresh Ubuntu 26.04 install on a Pascal laptop:
.RS 4
.nf
\&./pascal\-llm steps
sudo ./pascal\-llm run driver          # reboots
sudo ./pascal\-llm run build\-tools
sudo ./pascal\-llm run cuda
\&./pascal\-llm run llama
sudo MODEL=/path/to/model.gguf ./pascal\-llm run services
sudo ./pascal\-llm run tailscale
sudo ./pascal\-llm run smoke\-test
\&./pascal\-llm status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Any OpenAI client then talks to \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:8080/v1\fR, or to your machine's \fBts.net\fR address from your other devices.
.SH FOR AGENTS
.PP
Start with \fB./pascal\-llm status\fR and \fB./pascal\-llm steps\fR. Run steps in order, one at a time, and read each script before running it: they install drivers, reboot and write system files, so each needs the person's go ahead. After a kernel update, run \fB./pascal\-llm check\fR before anything else. Use the API on port 8080 as an OpenAI compatible model endpoint; never expose it with \fBtailscale funnel\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
https://github.com/jpsanders/pascal-llm-stack
